.54 (p=0.001), Sensitivity=55.83%, Specificity=77.24%, PPV=76.47%, NPV=56.89%, Accuracy=65.03% |\n| ≤ 8                | 72 (44.2%)         | 95 (77.2%)         | Chi-square test=31.54 (p=0.001), Sensitivity=55.83%, Specificity=77.24%, PPV=76.47%, NPV=56.89%, Accuracy=65.03% |\"}}, {section_id:\"Bidart_2024__sec_014\", properties:{level:1, heading:\"Ethics approval and consent to participate\", paper_file_name:\"Bidart_2024\", text:\"This study: 'Mortality predictors in patients with suspected sepsis in the emergency department of a tertiary care hospital: a retrospective cohort study' was submitted, reviewed and approved by the Ethics Committee of the Hospital Moinhos de Vento de Porto Alegre (number of document: 4.589.956, CAAE: 21988819.6.0000.5330) on March 14th, 2021, and conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki. Confidentiality, privacy, and anonymity of the data collected will be guaranteed and this committee accepted the justification for the absence of the informed consent form.\"}}, {section_id:\"Bidart_2024__sec_015\", properties:{heading:\"Consent for publication\", level:1, paper_file_name:\"Bidart_2024\", text:\"Not applicable.\"}}, {section_id:\"Bidart_2024__sec_016\", properties:{heading:\"Competing interests\", level:1, paper_file_name:\"Bidart_2024\", text:\"The authors declare no competing interests.\"}}, {section_id:\"Bidart_2024__sec_017\", properties:{heading:\"Author details\", level:1, paper_file_name:\"Bidart_2024\", page:9, text:\"1 Emergency Department, Moinhos de Vento Hospital, 910, Ramiro Barcelos Street, Porto Alegre, Zip code 90035-001, Brazil 2 Internal Medicine Department, Moinhos de Vento Hospital, Ramiro Barcelos, 630, Porto Alegre 90035- 001, Brazil 3 Proadi Social Responsability, Moinhos de Vento Hospital, Ramiro Barcelos, 910, Porto Alegre 90035- 000, Brazil 4\n5Section of Infectious Diseases, Hospital de Clínicas de Porto Alegre, Universidade Federal do Rio Grande do Sul, Ramiro Barcelos 2400, Porto Alegre 90035-003, Brazil\nReceived: 11 January 2024 / Accepted: 9 June 2024\n\n<!-- page: 9 -->\"}}, {section_id:\"Bidart_2024__sec_018\", properties:{heading:\"References\", level:1, paper_file_name:\"Bidart_2024\", text:\"Fleischmann C, Scherag A, Adhikari NKJ, Hartog CS, Tsaganos T, Schlattmann P, et al. Assessment of Global Incidence and Mortality of Hospital-treated Sepsis. Curr Estimates Limitations. 2016;193(3):259-72.\nReinhart K, Daniels R, Kissoon N, Machado FR, Schachter RD, Finfer S. Recognizing Sepsis as a Global Health Priority - a WHO Resolution. N Engl J Med. 2017;377(5):414-7.\nRudd KE, Johnson SC, Agesa KM, Shackelford KA, Tsoi D, Kievlan DR, et al. Global, regional, and national sepsis incidence and mortality, 1990-2017: analysis for the global burden of Disease Study. Lancet. 2020;395(10219):200-11.\nSinger M, Deutschman CS, Seymour CW, Shankar-Hari M, Annane D, Bauer M, et al. The Third International Consensus definitions for Sepsis and septic shock (Sepsis-3). JAMA. 2016;315(8):801.\nSeymour CW, Liu VX, Iwashyna TJ, Brunkhorst FM, Rea TD, Scherag A, et al. Assessment of Clinical Criteria for Sepsis: for the Third International Consensus definitions for Sepsis and septic shock (Sepsis-3). JAMA. 2016;315(8):762.\nSong JU, Sin CK, Park HK, Shim SR, Lee J. Performance of the quick sequential (sepsis-related) organ failure Assessment score as a prognostic tool in infected patients outside the intensive care unit: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Crit Care. 2018;22(1):28.\nLoritz M, Busch HJ, Helbing T, Fink K. Prospective evaluation of the quickSOFA score as a screening for sepsis in the emergency department. Intern Emerg Med. 2020;15(4):685-93.\nFreund Y, Lemachatti N, Krastinova E, Van Laer M, Claessens YE, Avondo A, et al. Prognostic accuracy of Sepsis-3 criteria for In-Hospital mortality among patients with suspected infection presenting to the Emergency Department. JAMA. 2017;317(3):301.\nBone RC, Balk RA, Cerra FB, Dellinger RP , Fein AM, Knaus WA, et al. Definitions for Sepsis and Organ failure and guidelines for the use of innovative therapies in Sepsis. Chest. 1992;101(6):1644-55.\nAit-Oufella H, Lemoinne S, Boelle PY, Galbois A, Baudel JL, Lemant J, et al. Mottling score predicts survival in septic shock. Intensive Care Med. 2011;37(5):801-7.\nAit-Oufella H, Joffre J, Boelle PY, Galbois A, Bourcier S, Baudel JL, et al. Knee area tissue oxygen saturation is predictive of 14-day mortality in septic shock. Intensive Care Med. 2012;38(6):976-83.\nPhua J, Ngerng W, See K, Tay C, Kiong T, Lim H, et al. Characteristics and outcomes of culture-negative versus culture-positive severe sepsis. Crit Care. 2013;17(5):R202.\nYang L, Lin Y, Wang J, Song J, Wei B, Zhang X, et al. Comparison of clinical characteristics and outcomes between positive and negative blood culture septic patients: a retrospective cohort study. IDR. 2021;14:4191-205.\nCoudroy R, Jamet A, Frat JP , Veinstein A, Chatellier D, Goudet V, et al. Incidence and impact of skin mottling over the knee and its duration on outcome in critically ill patients. Intensive Care Med. 2015;41(3):452-9.\nDumas G, Lavillegrand JR, Joffre J, Bigé N, de-Moura EB, Baudel JL, et al. Mottling score is a strong predictor of 14-day mortality in septic patients whatever vasopressor doses and other tissue perfusion parameters. Crit Care. 2019;23(1):211.\nPaquette K, Sweet D, Stenstrom R, Stabler SN, Lawandi A, Akhter M, et al. Neither blood culture positivity nor Time to positivity is Associated with Mortality among patients presenting with severe manifestations of Sepsis: the FABLED cohort study. Open Forum Infect Dis. 2021;8(7):ofab321.\nRamos JGR, da Hora Passos R, Teixeira MB, Gobatto ALN, Coutinho RV dos, Caldas S. Prognostic ability of quick-SOFA across different age groups of patients with suspected infection outside the intensive care unit: a cohort study. J Crit Care. 2018;47:178-84.\nCanet E, Taylor DM, Khor R, Krishnan V, Bellomo R. qSOFA as predictor of mortality and prolonged ICU admission in Emergency Department patients with suspected infection. J Crit Care. 2018;48:118-23.\nBoillat-Blanco N, Mbarack Z, Samaka J, Mlaganile T, Mamin A, Genton B, et al. Prognostic value of quickSOFA as a predictor of 28-day mortality among febrile adult patients presenting to emergency departments in Dar Es Salaam, Tanzania. Nanayakkara PWB, organizador. PLoS ONE. 2018;13(6):e0197982.\nLee J, Song JU. Performance of a quick sofa-65 score as a rapid sepsis screening tool during initial emergency department assessment: a propensity score matching study. J Crit Care. 2020;55:1-8.\nFernando SM, Tran A, Taljaard M, Cheng W, Rochwerg B, Seely AJE, et al. Prognostic accuracy of the Quick Sequential Organ failure Assessment for Mortality in patients with suspected infection: a systematic review and Metaanalysis. Ann Intern Med. 2018;168(4):266.\nAsner SA, Desgranges F, Schrijver IT, Calandra T. Impact of the timeliness of antibiotic therapy on the outcome of patients with sepsis and septic shock. J Infect. 2021;82(5):125-34.\"}}, {section_id:\"Bidart_2024__sec_019\", properties:{heading:\"Publisher's Note\", level:1, paper_file_name:\"Bidart_2024\", text:\"Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.\n\n<!-- page: 5 -->\nTable 1 Characteristics of the patients with suspicion of sepsis\n\n| Characteristic a                             | Patient data          |\n|----------------------------------------------|-----------------------|\n| Sex (No. [%])                                |                       |\n| Female                                       | 344(51.73)            |\n| Male                                         | 321(48.27)            |\n| Age (mean [SD]) (yr)                         | 73.3(19.19)           |\n| No. (%)                                      |                       |\n| 18-64                                        | 163 (24.51)           |\n| ≥65 