mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"3b3bae60e1283dbc3b97cdb1\", properties:{predictors:\"Onset of infection within 48 h of admission\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"Onset of infection within 48 h of admission, No. (%) | 128358 (86) | 6022 (77) | 58187 (87) | 5993 (76) | 58156 (87)\", verifier_verdict:\"ok\", timing:\"Within 48 hours of admission\", is_significant:false, adjustment_kind:\"univariate\", paper_file_name:\"Seymour_2016\", anchor_page:6, effect_type:\"other\", cohort_id:\"Seymour 2016 UPMC Validation ICU subset\", verifier_score:0.95, model_specification:\"Naive model, Descriptive characteristics of ICU encounters with suspected infection (validation cohort), Univariate analysis\", predictor_canonical:\"other\", outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"5993 (76%)\", anchor_section:\"Table 3. Characteristics of Encounters With Suspected Infection in the Primary Cohort at 12 UPMC Hospitals From 2010 to 2012 (N = 148 907)\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", outcome:\"In-hospital mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"b8f8c4599905ea8efc3dfa0c\", properties:{predictors:\"Pseudomonas infection\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"After adjusting for potential confounders, having no comorbid conditions and the use of antibiotics within 1 h of sepsis onset were associated with relatively lower ICU mortality in patients with sepsis. However, advanced age; high APACHE II score; comorbid cardiovascular diseases and tumors; infection with Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas, and Staphylococcus; and the use of RRT were independent risk factors for ICU mortality in patients with sepsis.\", verifier_verdict:\"partial\", timing:\"At onset of sepsis\", is_significant:false, adjustment_kind:\"multivariate\", paper_file_name:\"Wang_2020\", anchor_page:5, effect_type:\"OR\", cohort_id:\"Wang 2020 CCCST  Sepsis subgroup\", verifier_score:0.72, model_specification:\"Multivariable stepwise logistic regression adjusted for age, sex, APACHE II score, SOFA-initial score, comorbid conditions, use of MV, RRT and vasopressors, sites of infection, isolated organisms, use of antibiotics within 1h, use of antibiotics pre-sepsis, and septic shock\", predictor_canonical:\"pseudomonas\", outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"infection with Pseudomonas was independent risk factor for ICU mortality in patients with sepsis\", anchor_section:\"Factors Associated With Mortality in All Patients With Sepsis\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", outcome:\"ICU mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"a886d7b309540cec74fbb169\", properties:{predictors:\"SIRS, SOFA, LODS, qSOFA\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"Encounters in the ICU with 2 or more vs less than 2 SIRS criteria were compared with in decile of baseline risk and observed a 1- to 2-fold increased rate of hospital mortality compared with a 3- to 11-fold increase in mortality comparing those with 2 or more vs less than 2 SOFA points\", verifier_verdict:\"ok\", timing:\"Maximum score from 48 hours before to 24 hours after onset of infection\", is_significant:false, paper_file_name:\"Seymour_2016\", anchor_page:5, effect_type:\"other\", cohort_id:\"Seymour 2016 UPMC Validation ICU subset\", verifier_score:0.92, model_specification:\"Fold change in rate of in-hospital mortality comparing ≥2 vs <2 criteria across deciles of baseline risk, ICU encounters\", predictor_canonical:\"multivariable_model\", outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"1- to 2-fold increased rate of hospital mortality for SIRS ≥2 vs <2; 3- to 11-fold increase for SOFA ≥2 vs <2; fold change for LODS even greater than SOFA\", anchor_section:\"Performance of Existing Criteria in the ICU in the UPMC Cohort\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", outcome:\"In-hospital mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"ea5d3c627e41ff1ad74be75d\", properties:{predictors:\"qSOFA\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"The qSOFA agreed reasonably well with both SOFA (α = 0.73; 95% CI, 0.73-0.74) and LODS (α = 0.79; 95% CI, 0.780.79) and, unlike SOFA and LODS, also agreed more with SIRS (α = 0.69; 95% CI, 0.68-0.69)\", verifier_verdict:\"partial\", timing:\"Maximum score from 48 hours before to 24 hours after onset of infection\", is_significant:false, paper_file_name:\"Seymour_2016\", anchor_page:8, effect_type:\"other\", cohort_id:\"Seymour 2016 UPMC Validation ICU subset\", verifier_score:0.5, model_specification:\"Construct validity — Cronbach alpha agreement with SOFA, ICU encounters (n=7932)\", predictor_canonical:\"qSOFA\", outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"Cronbach α = 0.73 (95% CI, 0.73-0.74) with SOFA; α = 0.79 (95% CI, 0.78-0.79) with LODS; α = 0.69 (95% CI, 0.68-0.69) with SIRS\", anchor_section:\"Performance of New, Simple Criteria\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", outcome:\"In-hospital mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"276b9b1d9bbef82f2374e951\", properties:{predictors:\"Staphylococcus infection\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"Gram-positive (especially Staphylococcus) and gram-negative (Acinetobacter and Pseudomonas) organisms were associated with elevated mortality in patients with positive cultures, whereas Escherichia did not increase ICU mortality ( Table 6 ).\", verifier_verdict:\"ok\", timing:\"At onset of sepsis\", is_significant:false, adjustment_kind:\"univariate\", paper_file_name:\"Wang_2020\", anchor_page:5, effect_type:\"other\", cohort_id:\"Wang 2020 CCCST  Sepsis subgroup\", verifier_score:0.92, model_specification:\"Univariate analysis of ICU mortality in patients with positive cultures (Table 6)\", predictor_canonical:\"staphylococcus\", outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"Gram-positive (especially Staphylococcus) organisms were associated with elevated mortality in patients with positive cultures\", anchor_section:\"Factors Associated With Mortality in All Patients With Sepsis\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", outcome:\"ICU mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"004a362f45f990a386ffd7ac\", properties:{predictors:\"Staphylococcus infection\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"After adjusting for potential confounders, having no comorbid conditions and the use of antibiotics within 1 h of sepsis onset were associated with relatively lower ICU mortality in patients with sepsis. However, advanced age; high APACHE II score; comorbid cardiovascular diseases and tumors; infection with Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas, and Staphylococcus; and the use of RRT were independent risk factors for ICU mortality in patients with sepsis.\", verifier_verdict:\"partial\", timing:\"At onset of sepsis\", is_significant:false, adjustment_kind:\"multivariate\", paper_file_name:\"Wang_2020\", anchor_page:5, effect_type:\"OR\", cohort_id:\"Wang 2020 CCCST  Sepsis subgroup\", verifier_score:0.5, model_specification:\"Multivariable stepwise logistic regression adjusted for age, sex, APACHE II score, SOFA-initial score, comorbid conditions, use of MV, RRT and vasopressors, sites of infection, isolated organisms, use of antibiotics within 1h, use of antibiotics pre-sepsis, and septic shock\", predictor_canonical:\"staphylococcus\", outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"infection with Staphylococcus was independent risk factor for ICU mortality in patients with sepsis\", anchor_section:\"Factors Associated With Mortality in All Patients With Sepsis\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", outcome:\"ICU mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"1a33dc92220d66925d775b93\", properties:{predictors:\"Escherichia infection\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"Gram-positive (especially Staphylococcus) and gram-negative (Acinetobacter and Pseudomonas) organisms were associated with elevated mortality in patients with positive cultures, whereas Escherichia did not increase ICU mortality ( Table 6 ).\", verifier_verdict:\"partial\", timing:\"At onset of sepsis\", is_significant:false, adjustment_kind:\"univariate\", paper_file_name:\"Wang_2020\", anchor_page:5, effect_type:\"other\", cohort_id:\"Wang 2020 CCCST  Sepsis subgroup\", verifier_score:0.5, model_specification:\"Univariate analysis of ICU mortality in patients with positive cultures (Table 6)\", predictor_canonical:\"escherichia\", outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"Escherichia did not increase ICU mortality\", anchor_section:\"Factors Associated With Mortality in All Patients With Sepsis\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", outcome:\"ICU mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"f6c147767cdb1eb6dc12e01a\", properties:{predictors:\"No comorbid conditions\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"After adjusting for potential confounders, having no comorbid conditions and the use of antibiotics within 1 h of sepsis onset were associated with relatively lower ICU mortality in patients with sepsis.\", verifier_verdict:\"partial\", timing:\"Pre-existing on admission\", is_significant:false, adjustment_kind:\"multivariate\", paper_file_name:\"Wang_2020\", anchor_page:5, effect_type:\"OR\", cohort_id:\"Wang 2020 CCCST  Sepsis subgroup\", verifier_score:0.5, model_specification:\"Multivariable stepwise logistic regression adjusted for age, sex, APACHE II score, SOFA-initial score, comorbid conditions, use of MV, RRT and vasopressors, sites of infection, isolated organisms, use of antibiotics within 1h, use of antibiotics pre-sepsis, and septic shock\", predictor_canonical:\"no_comorbidities\", outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"having no comorbid conditions was associated with relatively lower ICU mortality in patients with sepsis\", anchor_section:\"Factors Associated With Mortality in All Patients With Sepsis\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", outcome:\"ICU mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"7dc5ce29086c5e2cad7f0384\", properties:{predictors:\"Antibiotics within 1 hour of sepsis onset\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"After adjusting for potential confounders, having no comorbid conditions and the use of antibiotics within 1 h of sepsis onset were associated with relatively lower ICU mortality in patients with sepsis.\", verifier_verdict:\"partial\", timing:\"Within 1 hour of sepsis onset\", is_significant:false, adjustment_kind:\"multivariate\", paper_file_name:\"Wang_2020\", anchor_page:5, effect_type:\"OR\", cohort_id:\"Wang 2020 CCCST  Sepsis subgroup\", verifier_score:0.5, model_specification:\"Multivariable stepwise logistic regression adjusted for age, sex, APACHE II score, SOFA-initial score, comorbid conditions, use of MV, RRT and vasopressors, sites of infection, isolated organisms, use of antibiotics within 1h, use of antibiotics pre-sepsis, and septic shock\", predictor_canonical:\"antibiotics_within_1h\", outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"use of antibiotics within 1 h of sepsis onset was associated with relatively lower ICU mortality in patients with sepsis\", anchor_section:\"Factors Associated With Mortality in All Patients With Sepsis\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", outcome:\"ICU mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"bfbf4f6b672ebb3ffa4f905a\", properties:{predictors:\"Antibiotics within 1 hour of sepsis onset\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"Our results show that initial antibiotic administration within 1 h of the onset of sepsis decreased ICU mortality by 42% in patients with sepsis (OR 0.58, 95% CI: 0.42-0.81)\", verifier_verdict:\"ok\", timing:\"Within 1 hour of sepsis onset\", is_significant:true, adjustment_kind:\"multivariate\", paper_file_name:\"Wang_2020\", effect_value:0.58, anchor_page:6, effect_type:\"OR\", cohort_id:\"Wang 2020 CCCST  Sepsis subgroup\", verifier_score:1.0, model_specification:\"Multivariable logistic regression (Table 7) adjusted for age, APACHE II score, SOFA-initial score, comorbid cardiovascular diseases, hypertension, diabetes mellitus and chronic renal failure, use of MV, RRT and vasopressors, administration of antibiotics within 1h, and 30 mL/kg fluid resuscitation within the first 3h\", predictor_canonical:\"antibiotics_within_1h\", ci_lo:0.42, outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"OR 0.58 (95% CI: 0.42-0.81)\", anchor_section:\"DISCUSSION\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", ci_hi:0.81, outcome:\"ICU mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"5d9f232f3a04406ad23f3e65\", properties:{predictors:\"Antibiotics within 1 hour of sepsis onset\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"the prompt administration of antibiotics (OR 0.65, 95% CI 0.46-0.92) and 30 mL/kg of initial fluid resuscitation during the first 3 h (OR 0.43, 95% CI 0.30-0.63) improved the outcome in patients with septic shock.\", verifier_verdict:\"ok\", timing:\"Within 1 hour of sepsis onset\", is_significant:true, adjustment_kind:\"multivariate\", paper_file_name:\"Wang_2020\", effect_value:0.65, anchor_page:1, effect_type:\"OR\", cohort_id:\"Wang 2020 CCCST  Sepsis subgroup\", verifier_score:1.0, model_specification:\"Multivariable logistic regression (Table 7) — sepsis patients, adjusted for age, sex, APACHE II score, SOFA-initial score, comorbid conditions, use of MV, RRT and vasopressors, sites of infection, isolated organisms, use of antibiotics within 1h, use of antibiotics pre-sepsis, and septic shock\", predictor_canonical:\"antibiotics_within_1h\", ci_lo:0.46, outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"OR 0.65 (95% CI 0.46-0.92)\", anchor_section:\"The Prevalence, Risk Factors, and Outcomes of Sepsis in Critically Ill Patients in China: A Multicenter Prospective Cohort Study\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", ci_hi:0.92, outcome:\"ICU mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}] AS row
