pecially Staphylococcus) and gram-negative (Acinetobacter and Pseudomonas) organisms were associated with elevated mortality in patients with positive cultures, whereas Escherichia did not increase ICU mortality ( Table 6 ).\", verifier_verdict:\"partial\", timing:\"At onset of sepsis\", is_significant:false, adjustment_kind:\"univariate\", paper_file_name:\"Wang_2020\", anchor_page:5, effect_type:\"other\", cohort_id:\"Wang 2020 CCCST Sepsis subgroup\", verifier_score:0.5, model_specification:\"Univariate analysis of association with ICU mortality in patients with positive cultures (Table 6)\", predictor_canonical:\"Escherichia\", outcome_type:\"mortality\", effect_size_str:\"Did not increase ICU mortality\", anchor_section:\"Factors Associated With Mortality in All Patients With Sepsis\", prompt_id:\"predictor_extract_v1@618f1f75\", outcome:\"ICU mortality\", anchor_bbox:\"null\"}}, {id:\"6ba7f6b04c3b60ac5c5e8d27\", properties:{predictors:\"age, APACHE II score, SOFA-initial score, comorbid cardiovascular diseases, malignant tumors, renal replacement therapy, septic shock, Acinetobacter infection, Pseudomonas infection, Staphylococcus infection, no comorbid conditions, antibiotics within 1h of sepsis onset\", extractor_model:\"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6\", anchor_text:\"After adjusting for potential confounders, having no comorbid conditions and the use of antibiotics within 1 h of sepsis onset were associated with relatively lower ICU mortality in patients with sepsis. However, advanced age; high APACHE II score; comorbid cardiovascular diseases and tumors; infection with Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas, and Staphylococcus; and the use of RRT were independent risk factors for ICU mortality in patients with sepsis.\", verifier_verdict:\"partial\", timing:\"On admission to ICU or within first 48h\", is_significant:false, adjustment_kind:\"multivariate\", paper_file_name:\"Wang_2020\", anchor_page:5, effect_type:\"other\", cohort_id:\"Wang 2020 CCCST Sepsis subgroup\", 